# ULTRON notification configuration.
#
#   cp .notify.example .notify
#   chmod 600 .notify
#
# This file is configuration. The password is a secret and does NOT belong here:
# it lives in .env, which is also gitignored and which start.sh sources before it
# reads this file.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#   chmod 600 .env
#   # then, in .env:  ULTRON_SMTP_PASS='your app password'
#
# .notify is gitignored and stays on this machine. Every value can also be
# exported in the environment, and the environment wins over this file — useful
# for CI, or for a systemd unit that reads its secrets from elsewhere:
#
#   ULTRON_SMTP_HOST  ULTRON_SMTP_PORT  ULTRON_SMTP_USER
#   ULTRON_SMTP_PASS  ULTRON_MAIL_FROM  ULTRON_MAIL_TO
#   ULTRON_PUSH_URL   ULTRON_PUSH_TOPIC ULTRON_PUSH_BANDS
#
# Both channels are optional and independent. With neither configured the alert
# manager still runs: it records every alert and every band change to the
# evidence vault and logs "[mail suppressed]" / "[push suppressed]" instead of
# sending. A notification is layered on top of the record, never a prerequisite
# for it — a dead relay must not cost you the evidence.
#
# Phase 1 notifies on RED and PURPLE only. YELLOW is recorded and shown but
# never wakes anyone: a notification that fires on YELLOW trains the operator
# to ignore it.

# --- mail ------------------------------------------------------------------
# Port 587 with STARTTLS is what this client speaks. Port 465 (implicit TLS)
# is NOT supported: there is no SMTP_SSL path in the node.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587

# Leave USER empty for a relay that takes no authentication (a LAN mail server,
# or a test relay). Set it, and the client upgrades the connection with STARTTLS
# *before* it presents the password.
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

MAIL_FROM=ultron@localhost
# One or more recipients, comma- or space-separated.
MAIL_TO=

# --- push ------------------------------------------------------------------
# An ntfy-compatible server. Leave PUSH_TOPIC empty and no push is ever sent:
# nothing is dialled and no alert content leaves this machine.
#
# The topic name is the whole secret. Anyone who knows it can read your alerts,
# so make it long and random and treat it like a password. Generate one with:
#
#   openssl rand -hex 16
#
# Point PUSH_URL at your own server (an ntfy on the Pi4) to keep alert traffic
# on the LAN. ntfy.sh is the convenient default, and the only reason this file
# offers it is that it works without a second machine standing up tonight.
PUSH_URL=https://ntfy.sh
PUSH_TOPIC=

# Which bands push. Widening this to YELLOW,RED,PURPLE is fine for a demo; on a
# wall-mounted appliance it is how you train yourself to ignore it.
PUSH_BANDS=RED,PURPLE

# --- Gmail example ---------------------------------------------------------
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=you@gmail.com
# MAIL_TO=you@gmail.com
# ...and the app password goes in .env, never here:
#   ULTRON_SMTP_PASS='abcd efgh ijkl mnop'   # spaces are fine; Google shows groups of 4
#
# Gmail wants an app password (turn on 2-Step Verification first). The account
# password is rejected with a 535, which this client logs as
# "could not send notification:" and otherwise ignores — by design, so a dead
# mail relay can never stop the vault being written. Check the log, not the exit
# code, when you rehearse.
#
# The relay's certificate IS verified. A self-signed LAN mail server therefore
# needs its CA trusted by the process — point SSL_CERT_FILE at the cert, or add
# it to the system trust store.

# --- phone example ---------------------------------------------------------
# PUSH_TOPIC=ultron-7f3c9a1e5b2d8460
#
# Install the ntfy app (iOS / Android), subscribe to the same topic, and a RED
# or PURPLE band arrives as a phone notification a second or two after the
# dashboard changes colour. Subscribe BEFORE the demo runs, and send yourself a
# test push from the ntfy web UI to prove the app is actually receiving.
